## ===========================================================================
## THE DISGUISE — the dress and hat, the change behind the stall, the boots,
## and the way out
##
## The escape's shape (change document, "The escape disguise", with its
## 2026-08-23/24 amendments): necklace → Teisha → the dress and the fashionable
## hat into the satchel → back up the post → slide on the gray cloak → let go
## mid-wire → Behind Fruit Stall → change → out to the Fruit Stall → east.
##
## THE LOOK IS A STATE, NOT A SET OF OBJECTS (David, 2026-08-24: "all clothing
## is scenery and not directly removable"). Jack carries `states: urchin,
## dressed, identified` in `secret-letter.story`. The dress and hat are items
## only until the change; CHANGE OUTFIT consumes them into the look — they
## leave play, the cap goes into the satchel, and Jack is `dressed`. The
## gray cloak already lies wherever the slide left it. Nothing is worn or
## taken off piece by piece.
##
## THE WINDOWS, in turns (change document, "Both timing questions"):
##
##   1. `let go` mid-wire drops her Behind Fruit Stall and starts the
##      player's `lingering` clock (one named turn, `changing`).
##   2. The next command is the change. If she is STILL behind the stall on
##      the turn after that, `lingering` expires there: the mercenaries move
##      to the Fruit Stall and stepping out is death (the clause lives on
##      Behind Fruit Stall in `aerial-runway.chord`).
##   3. Coming out to the Fruit Stall while `dressed` starts the player's
##      `exposure` clock (no named turns — it expires on the following
##      turn). Leave the stall on that turn and the head start holds; stay,
##      and the boots-spotted beat fires: Jack becomes `identified`, the
##      mercenaries arrive knowing what she wears, and the sweep reads her
##      again.
##
## RECOGNITION (change document, "The recognition rule, sharpened"): the sweep
## reads Jack in every state except `dressed`. `after going while hunted` in
## Jack's block restarts `waiting` only while she is not `dressed`; once
## `identified` it reads her again. No suppression is keyed to the cap.
##
## The east exit is HELD for David beyond the placeholders below: the
## source's Eastern Junction rules (`story.ni:2481-2496`) are the cap-off-era
## captain text the recognition ruling supersedes, kept aside in the comment
## at the bottom of this file.
##
## Every finished line below is Gentry's; every TODO is David's.
## ===========================================================================

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE GARMENTS — Teisha's, until the trade (`npc-teisha.chord` gives them)
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

## DAVID: the dress replaces the source's green silk cloak (`story.ni:3061`,
## "a simple drape of soft, dusky green with silver trim and a voluminous
## hood"); no dress text exists in 2009. Name, colour, and description are
## yours — the noun `dress` and the aliases below are structural stand-ins.

create the dress
  aka gown, green dress, new dress

  (TODO during play-testing — A simple dress of dusky green, cut for a lady of
  no great means but some taste, with a little silver stitching at the collar.
  Teisha said it would suit you. Standing here in your rags, you can't imagine
  how.)

  on the player wearing
    refuse change-outfit-instead
  end on

## DAVID: the fashionable hat is the remake's own object. `hat` alone also
## names the woolen cap, so the parser will ask which while both exist.

create the fashionable hat
  aka fashionable hat, new hat, lady's hat, bonnet

  (TODO during play-testing — A lady's hat with a brim wide enough to hide a
  face under, trimmed to match the dress. It would look ridiculous over a
  woolen cap.)

  on the player wearing
    refuse change-outfit-instead
  end on

## Wearing either garment directly is redirected to the outfit change (the
## clothing-is-the-look ruling). Line is David's.

define phrase change-outfit-instead
  (TODO during play-testing — You can't just pull a dress on over an urchin's
  rags. If you're going to do this, do it properly: somewhere out of sight,
  the whole outfit at once.)
end phrase

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE CHANGE — one action, one turn, Behind Fruit Stall only
##
## Grammar carries the outfit-change phrasings (CHANGE OUTFIT, SWITCH HATS)
## plus the plain ones a player types. The words are LITERAL: in a grammar
## line `the <word>` declares a slot (`kick the target`), so `wear the
## dress` would have bound any reachable thing as "the dress" and made the
## body's `the dress` a slot reference (unmet on `change outfit` — ADR-275
## D6). WEAR DRESS is deliberately NOT here: a story grammar line outranks
## the standard one (ADR-268 D2) for the whole game, so `wear dress` as an
## action pattern shadowed stdlib `wearing` in every chapter — Chapter 8's
## day dress could not be worn by that phrase (GH #374). Wearing stays
## stdlib's; the `on the player wearing` intercepts above are the story's
## hook and redirect the escape dress and hat to the outfit change.
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

define action changing-outfit
  grammar
    change outfit
    change clothes
    switch hats
  the player must have the dress: change-no-dress
  refuse when the player is not in Behind Fruit Stall: change-not-here
  refuse when Jack is not urchin: change-already
  phrase change-outfit
  change Jack to dressed
  move the dress offstage
  move the fashionable hat offstage
  move the woolen cap to the cloth satchel

## DAVID: the change itself — the one turn behind the stall. The source's
## nearest line is the cloak's (`story.ni:3087`): "You slip the silk cloak
## over your shoulders and pull the hood up. The fabric feels smooth and
## cool on your skin, but you don't have time to admire it now — you've got
## to run!" — kept aside; the dress and hat need their own.

define phrase change-outfit
  (TODO during play-testing — Behind the stall, with the crates for a screen,
  you strip off the cap and the rags and wriggle into the dress. The hat goes
  on over your cropped hair; the cap goes into the satchel. You smooth the
  skirt with both hands, and for a moment you don't know what to do with your
  feet. Then you remember. You're a lady. Ladies walk.)
end phrase

define phrase change-no-dress
  (TODO during play-testing — You have nothing to change into. Rags and a
  woolen cap are the whole of your wardrobe.)
end phrase

define phrase change-not-here
  (TODO during play-testing — Not here, in front of half the market. You need
  somewhere out of sight.)
end phrase

define phrase change-already
  (TODO during play-testing — You're already wearing the dress. Once is
  plenty.)
end phrase

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE CLOCKS
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

## One turn to change, one turn to return (change document, amended
## 2026-08-23). Started by `let go` / `drop` in `aerial-runway.chord`.

define timer lingering for the player
  changing
end timer

## One turn at the Fruit Stall before identification (change document, "Both
## timing questions", 1). Started by the Fruit Stall's own `after the player
## entering while Jack is dressed` clause (`grubbers-market.chord`) — on the
## room itself, not a composed trait: a trait's `entering` clause did not
## fire for the room that carried it (2026-08-29).

define timer exposure for the player
end timer

